[38;5;252m[1m## The bug[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mrun(NULL, n)[0m behaved differently across the FFI boundary:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
| | C | Rust (before) |[0m[0m
|---|---|---|[0m[0m
| [38;5;10mrun(NULL, 1)[0m | SIGSEGV (signal 11) | SIGABRT (signal 6) — [38;5;10mnull pointer dereference occurred[0m |[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust [38;5;10mrun[0m did [38;5;10mlet house = &mut *the_house;[0m. Forming a Rust reference from a raw pointer (and, after my first attempt, even a plain [38;5;10m(*p).field[0m deref) is instrumented under [38;5;10m-C debug-assertions[0m, so rustc's UB check converted the fault into a non-unwinding panic and [38;5;10mabort()[0m. The C library has no NULL guard at all and simply faults.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The fix routes every [38;5;10mhouse->field[0m access through libc [38;5;10mmemcpy[0m on a byte-offset address ([38;5;10mcore::mem::offset_of![0m), so address arithmetic never dereferences in Rust and the faulting load/store happens inside libc. Behaviour is now identical in [3mboth[23m cargo profiles — the release build happened to segfault already, but the debug build did not, and I did not want that caveat.[0m[0m
